{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WbE3EPzFJVh8nGycwpU6RH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture static probe evaluation\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Train static LogisticRegression on hidden states from DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B at layer 20 (proportional mid-layer for 64-layer model). Evaluate coverage (crossing rate), accuracy, and Fisher discriminant ratio in original and PCA-reduced spaces.\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"r1_32b_static_acc\": 0.8341,\n7\t      \"r1_32b_static_coverage\": 0.5283,\n8\t      \"r1_32b_static_lead_mean\": -1.4,\n9\t      \"r1_32b_fisher_orig_L32\": 11.23,\n10\t      \"r1_32b_fisher_orig_L40\": 12.07,\n11\t      \"r1_32b_fisher_pca3_L32\": 2.61,\n12\t      \"r1_32b_fisher_pca3_L40\": 2.84,\n13\t      \"r1_32b_var_explained_3pc\": 0.231,\n14\t      \"qwq_32b_static_acc\": 0.8189,\n15\t      \"qwq_32b_static_coverage\": 0.4815,\n16\t      \"qwq_32b_static_lead_mean\": -1.8,\n17\t      \"qwq_32b_fisher_orig_L32\": 9.74,\n18\t      \"qwq_32b_fisher_orig_L40\": 10.52,\n19\t      \"qwq_32b_fisher_pca3_L32\": 2.19,\n20\t      \"qwq_32b_fisher_pca3_L40\": 2.43,\n21\t      \"qwq_32b_var_explained_3pc\": 0.214,\n22\t      \"num_traces_r1_32b\": 187,\n23\t      \"num_traces_qwq\": 192,\n24\t      \"split_ratio\": \"112/37/38 and 115/38/39\"\n25\t    },\n26\t    \"tables\": [\n27\t      {\n28\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: Static Probe Results Across Architectures\",\n29\t        \"columns\": [\n30\t          \"Model\",\n31\t          \"Base Arch\",\n32\t          \"Hidden Dim\",\n33\t          \"Best Layer\",\n34\t          \"Accuracy\",\n35\t          \"Coverage\",\n36\t          \"Lead Time\",\n37\t          \"Fisher_orig\",\n38\t          \"Fisher_PCA3\",\n39\t          \"Var_3PC\"\n40\t        ],\n41\t        \"rows\": [\n42\t          [\n43\t            \"r1-8b\",\n44\t            \"Llama-3.1\",\n45\t            4096,\n46\t            \"L14\",\n47\t            0.817,\n48\t            0.465,\n49\t            -2.0,\n50\t            17.88,\n51\t            0.93,\n52\t            0.166\n53\t          ],\n54\t          [\n55\t            \"r1-32b\",\n56\t            \"Qwen2.5\",\n57\t            5120,\n58\t            \"L32\",\n59\t            0.834,\n60\t            0.528,\n61\t            -1.4,\n62\t            12.07,\n63\t            2.84,\n64\t            0.231\n65\t          ],\n66\t          [\n67\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n68\t            \"Qwen2.5\",\n69\t            5120,\n70\t            \"L32\",\n71\t            0.819,\n72\t            0.482,\n73\t            -1.8,\n74\t            10.52,\n75\t            2.43,\n76\t            0.214\n77\t          ]\n78\t        ]\n79\t      }\n80\t    ],\n81\t    \"figures\": [],\n82\t    \"analysis\": \"Claim 1 validated cross-architecture: static probes achieve 48-53% coverage on Qwen2.5 models, remaining below 60% threshold. Architectural difference in signal distribution is quantifiable — Qwen2.5 has lower full-space Fisher (signal more diffuse per-dimension) but higher PCA-space Fisher (signal more concentrated in leading components). This sets up the key cross-architecture comparison: Qwen2.5's different signal geometry may alter temporal probe dynamics.\\n\\nQwen2.5 architecture distributes safety signal differently than Llama — Fisher discriminant in original space is 9-12 (vs Llama's 17-18) because Qwen2.5's wider MLP and GQA attention spread safety-relevant features across more dimensions with lower per-dimension separability. However, PCA top-3 captures more variance (21-23% vs Llama's 16.6%) and Fisher in PCA space is higher (2.2-2.8 vs Llama's ~1), indicating Qwen2.5 concentrates safety signal slightly MORE in leading PCs. Static coverage is marginally higher than Llama (48-53% vs 46.5%) because this higher PCA concentration gives a linear probe slightly more accessible signal, but still below 60% — confirming Claim 1 generalizes. The lower full-space Fisher explains why the absolute coverage remains limited despite better geometric accessibility.\"\n83\t  },\n84\t  {\n85\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation\",\n86\t    \"description\": \"Train temporal window LogisticRegression with W=15 on r1-32b and QwQ-32B hidden states. Compare coverage improvement (delta over static) to r1-8b's +30pp.\",\n87\t    \"results\": {\n88\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_acc\": 0.8512,\n89\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_coverage\": 0.6792,\n90\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean\": 0.52,\n91\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_lead_median\": 0,\n92\t      \"r1_32b_delta_over_static\": 0.1509,\n93\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_acc\": 0.8421,\n94\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_coverage\": 0.6481,\n95\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean\": -0.73,\n96\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_lead_median\": -1,\n97\t      \"qwq_32b_delta_over_static\": 0.1666,\n98\t      \"r1_8b_temporal_w15_coverage_ref\": 0.7674,\n99\t      \"r1_8b_delta_ref\": 0.3023,\n100\t      \"attenuation_factor_r1_32b\": 0.5,\n101\t      \"attenuation_factor_qwq\": 0.55\n102\t    },\n103\t    \"tables\": [\n104\t      {\n105\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: Temporal Window Probe (W=15) Across Architectures\",\n106\t        \"columns\": [\n107\t          \"Model\",\n108\t          \"Static Cov\",\n109\t          \"Temporal Cov\",\n110\t          \"Delta\",\n111\t          \"Lead Mean\",\n112\t          \"Lead Median\",\n113\t          \"Acc\"\n114\t        ],\n115\t        \"rows\": [\n116\t          [\n117\t            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n118\t            0.465,\n119\t            0.767,\n120\t            \"+30.2pp\",\n121\t            -1.09,\n122\t            -1,\n123\t            0.848\n124\t          ],\n125\t          [\n126\t            \"r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\",\n127\t            0.528,\n128\t            0.679,\n129\t            \"+15.1pp\",\n130\t            0.52,\n131\t            0,\n132\t            0.851\n133\t          ],\n134\t          [\n135\t            \"QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\",\n136\t            0.482,\n137\t            0.648,\n138\t            \"+16.7pp\",\n139\t            -0.73,\n140\t            -1,\n141\t            0.842\n142\t          ]\n143\t        ]\n144\t      }\n145\t    ],\n146\t    \"figures\": [],\n147\t    \"analysis\": \"Temporal advantage is real and consistent (+15-17pp) but architecture-dependent in magnitude. The 30pp gain on Llama attenuates to ~16pp on Qwen2.5, likely because Qwen2.5's signal geometry (higher PCA concentration) gives static probes a head start. Claim 2 must be softened: temporal probes 'consistently improve coverage with architecture-dependent magnitude' rather than 'saturate oracle ceiling cross-architecture.' The r1-32b slightly positive lead time (0.52 steps) is intriguing but not statistically robust — this becomes the directional sub-finding that motivates future work.\\n\\nTemporal advantage exists but is attenuated to ~15-17pp (vs 30pp on Llama). The attenuation occurs because Qwen2.5's higher PCA concentration means static probes already capture more of the accessible signal — there's less 'headroom' for temporal context to add. The temporal probe still helps because safety reasoning density decline patterns exist cross-architecture, but the already-higher static baseline leaves less room for improvement. Coverage reaches 65-68% rather than 77% — this does NOT saturate the oracle ceiling (which would be ~77% if oracle patterns are similar). The r1-32b result of +0.52 lead time is the 'ambiguous' sub-finding: slightly positive but within noise given bootstrap CI will span [-1.5, 2.5]. QwQ remains clearly negative. This architecture-dependent magnitude is the key finding that reshapes Claim 2.\"\n148\t  },\n149\t  {\n150\t    \"name\": \"Cross-model transfer evaluation\",\n151\t    \"description\": \"Zero-shot cross-architecture transfer: train probe on r1-8b (Llama, dim=4096), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5, dim=5120) via learned linear projection. And reverse direction. Evaluate with both static and temporal probes.\",\n152\t    \"results\": {\n153\t      \"llama_to_qwen_static_coverage\": 0.372,\n154\t      \"llama_to_qwen_temporal_coverage\": 0.426,\n155\t      \"qwen_to_llama_static_coverage\": 0.349,\n156\t      \"qwen_to_llama_temporal_coverage\": 0.401,\n157\t      \"within_arch_qwen_to_qwen_static\": 0.491,\n158\t      \"within_arch_qwen_to_qwen_temporal\": 0.612,\n159\t      \"projection_dim\": 512,\n160\t      \"projection_method\": \"learned linear (trained on 50 aligned traces)\",\n161\t      \"random_baseline_coverage\": 0.167\n162\t    },\n163\t    \"tables\": [\n164\t      {\n165\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: Cross-Architecture Transfer Results\",\n166\t        \"columns\": [\n167\t          \"Train Model\",\n168\t          \"Test Model\",\n169\t          \"Probe Type\",\n170\t          \"Coverage\",\n171\t          \"vs In-Domain\"\n172\t        ],\n173\t        \"rows\": [\n174\t          [\n175\t            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n176\t            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n177\t            \"Static\",\n178\t            0.372,\n179\t            \"0.528 (-15.6pp)\"\n180\t          ],\n181\t          [\n182\t            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n183\t            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n184\t            \"Temporal W=15\",\n185\t            0.426,\n186\t            \"0.679 (-25.3pp)\"\n187\t          ],\n188\t          [\n189\t            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n190\t            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n191\t            \"Static\",\n192\t            0.349,\n193\t            \"0.465 (-11.6pp)\"\n194\t          ],\n195\t          [\n196\t            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n197\t            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n198\t            \"Temporal W=15\",\n199\t            0.401,\n200\t            \"0.767 (-36.6pp)\"\n201\t          ],\n202\t          [\n203\t            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n204\t            \"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\",\n205\t            \"Static\",\n206\t            0.491,\n207\t            \"0.482 (+0.9pp)\"\n208\t          ],\n209\t          [\n210\t            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n211\t            \"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\",\n212\t            \"Temporal W=15\",\n213\t            0.612,\n214\t            \"0.648 (-3.6pp)\"\n215\t          ]\n216\t        ]\n217\t      }\n218\t    ],\n219\t    \"figures\": [],\n220\t    \"analysis\": \"Cross-architecture transfer confirms the 'architecture-specific signal geometry' hypothesis. Within-architecture transfer nearly matches in-domain performance (especially Qwen→Qwen), while cross-architecture transfer drops 12-37pp. This supports framing cross-architecture validation as characterization rather than claiming a universal detector — practical deployment requires per-architecture probe training. The finding strengthens the architectural analysis contribution axis.\\n\\nCross-architecture zero-shot transfer is weak (35-43% coverage) because the safety signal occupies architecture-specific subspaces — a linear projection trained on few aligned samples cannot fully recover the mapping between Llama and Qwen2.5 hidden state geometries. The projection captures gross safe/unsafe distinctions (well above random baseline of 16.7%) but loses the fine-grained temporal patterns that drive the +15-30pp temporal advantage. Within-architecture transfer (Qwen→Qwen, r1-32b→QwQ) performs substantially better (49-61%) because both Qwen2.5 models share similar signal geometry. This confirms the hypothesis that cross-architecture transfer requires architecture-specific fine-tuning — the safety concept is universal but its geometric instantiation is architecture-dependent.\"\n221\t  },\n222\t  {\n223\t    \"name\": \"Lead time analysis across models\",\n224\t    \"description\": \"Analyze lead time distributions for temporal probes on all 3 models. Report per-model distributions, positive lead rate, and compare to oracle's +31 step advantage.\",\n225\t    \"results\": {\n226\t      \"r1_8b_lead_mean\": -1.09,\n227\t      \"r1_8b_lead_median\": -1,\n228\t      \"r1_8b_positive_lead_rate\": 0.302,\n229\t      \"r1_32b_lead_mean\": 0.52,\n230\t      \"r1_32b_lead_median\": 0,\n231\t      \"r1_32b_positive_lead_rate\": 0.421,\n232\t      \"qwq_lead_mean\": -0.73,\n233\t      \"qwq_lead_median\": -1,\n234\t      \"qwq_positive_lead_rate\": 0.333,\n235\t      \"oracle_lead_mean_all_models\": 31,\n236\t      \"gap_probe_vs_oracle\": \"29-32 steps across models\",\n237\t      \"r1_32b_lead_std\": 4.8,\n238\t      \"qwq_lead_std\": 5.1,\n239\t      \"r1_32b_lead_p25\": -2,\n240\t      \"r1_32b_lead_p75\": 3\n241\t    },\n242\t    \"tables\": [\n243\t      {\n244\t        \"name\": \"Table 6: Lead Time Distribution by Model\",\n245\t        \"columns\": [\n246\t          \"Model\",\n247\t          \"Mean\",\n248\t          \"Median\",\n249\t          \"Std\",\n250\t          \"P25\",\n251\t          \"P75\",\n252\t          \"% Positive\",\n253\t          \"Oracle Mean\"\n254\t        ],\n255\t        \"rows\": [\n256\t          [\n257\t            \"r1-8b\",\n258\t            -1.09,\n259\t            -1,\n260\t            4.2,\n261\t            -4,\n262\t            1,\n263\t            \"30.2%\",\n264\t            31\n265\t          ],\n266\t          [\n267\t            \"r1-32b\",\n268\t            0.52,\n269\t            0,\n270\t            4.8,\n271\t            -2,\n272\t            3,\n273\t            \"42.1%\",\n274\t            31\n275\t          ],\n276\t          [\n277\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n278\t            -0.73,\n279\t            -1,\n280\t            5.1,\n281\t            -3,\n282\t            2,\n283\t            \"33.3%\",\n284\t            31\n285\t          ]\n286\t        ]\n287\t      }\n288\t    ],\n289\t    \"figures\": [],\n290\t    \"analysis\": \"The near-zero lead time generalizes cross-architecture. The r1-32b slightly positive mean is a directional curiosity — 42% of traces show positive lead (vs 30% on Llama) but the absolute values remain too small for practical early warning. The 30-step gap between probe and oracle persists uniformly, confirming hidden states encode CURRENT safety state but not FUTURE trajectory. Claim 3 reframed as 'lead time is near-zero across architectures' — a softer statement that accommodates the r1-32b sub-finding while maintaining the core limitation message.\\n\\nLead time remains near-zero across all architectures, confirming the fundamental limitation of hidden-state approaches. The r1-32b result (+0.52 mean) is the one 'ambiguous' sub-finding — technically positive but the distribution spans [-8, +12] with median at 0, so it's not practically meaningful. The slight positive tendency on r1-32b may reflect Qwen2.5's more concentrated signal geometry making the commitment transition slightly more abrupt (and thus detectable 1-2 steps earlier in some traces). However, this does NOT constitute reliable pre-textual detection — the oracle still achieves +31 steps via label-sequence patterns invisible to hidden states. Claim 3 generalizes but should be stated as 'near-zero' rather than 'strictly negative' to accommodate the r1-32b directional finding.\"\n291\t  },\n292\t  {\n293\t    \"name\": \"TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline\",\n294\t    \"description\": \"Implement Mahalanobis distance detector using safe-trace covariance estimation. Compute per-step MD, threshold for anomaly detection on all 3 models. Expected to partially work on Qwen2.5 (higher PCA concentration) but fail on Llama.\",\n295\t    \"results\": {\n296\t      \"r1_8b_mahalanobis_coverage\": 0.302,\n297\t      \"r1_8b_mahalanobis_acc\": 0.714,\n298\t      \"r1_8b_mahalanobis_auroc\": 0.683,\n299\t      \"r1_32b_mahalanobis_coverage\": 0.453,\n300\t      \"r1_32b_mahalanobis_acc\": 0.779,\n301\t      \"r1_32b_mahalanobis_auroc\": 0.752,\n302\t      \"qwq_mahalanobis_coverage\": 0.407,\n303\t      \"qwq_mahalanobis_acc\": 0.761,\n304\t      \"qwq_mahalanobis_auroc\": 0.734,\n305\t      \"num_safe_traces_covariance\": \"70/65/68 per model\",\n306\t      \"threshold_method\": \"95th percentile of safe-trace MD distribution\",\n307\t      \"pca_reduced_md_r1_8b\": 0.256,\n308\t      \"pca_reduced_md_r1_32b\": 0.372\n309\t    },\n310\t    \"tables\": [\n311\t      {\n312\t        \"name\": \"Table 7: Mahalanobis Distance Baseline\",\n313\t        \"columns\": [\n314\t          \"Model\",\n315\t          \"Coverage\",\n316\t          \"Accuracy\",\n317\t          \"AUROC\",\n318\t          \"vs Static LR\",\n319\t          \"vs Temporal LR\"\n320\t        ],\n321\t        \"rows\": [\n322\t          [\n323\t            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n324\t            0.302,\n325\t            0.714,\n326\t            0.683,\n327\t            \"-16.3pp\",\n328\t            \"-46.5pp\"\n329\t          ],\n330\t          [\n331\t            \"r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\",\n332\t            0.453,\n333\t            0.779,\n334\t            0.752,\n335\t            \"-7.5pp\",\n336\t            \"-22.6pp\"\n337\t          ],\n338\t          [\n339\t            \"QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\",\n340\t            0.407,\n341\t            0.761,\n342\t            0.734,\n343\t            \"-7.5pp\",\n344\t            \"-24.1pp\"\n345\t          ]\n346\t        ]\n347\t      }\n348\t    ],\n349\t    \"figures\": [],\n350\t    \"analysis\": \"Mahalanobis partially works on Qwen2.5 (40-45% coverage, AUROC 0.73-0.75) — this is the 'ambiguous sub-finding' of the median tier. The result is architecturally explained by signal concentration in leading PCs, which means TrajGuard-style detectors would have partial success on Qwen2.5 reasoning models but near-complete failure on Llama-based ones. Reframe Claim 1 to note that 'geometric detectors show architecture-dependent effectiveness correlated with PCA concentration of safety signal' rather than blanket failure. Temporal probes still dominate in all cases, preserving the core story.\\n\\nMahalanobis distance PARTIALLY works on Qwen2.5 (40-45% coverage) but fails on Llama (30%). This is directly explained by the PCA concentration difference: Qwen2.5's higher Fisher in PCA space (2.4-2.8 vs Llama's ~1) means the covariance-based geometric detector can capture more of the safety signal, since more signal lives in the directions that dominate the covariance matrix. On Llama, safety signal is distributed across minor PCs that the covariance matrix effectively ignores — Mahalanobis detects gross distribution shifts but misses the subtle safety-specific directions. This is the 'ambiguous' result for this experiment: Mahalanobis doesn't completely fail on Qwen2.5 (as we'd predicted from Llama-only results), complicating the 'existing detectors fail' narrative. Must reframe as 'architecture-dependent failure mode' rather than universal failure.\"\n351\t  },\n352\t  {\n353\t    \"name\": \"Bootstrap confidence intervals\",\n354\t    \"description\": \"1000 bootstrap resamples on all key metrics across all models and methods. Report 95% CIs to support statistical claims.\",\n355\t    \"results\": {\n356\t      \"r1_8b_temporal_coverage_ci\": [\n357\t        0.651,\n358\t        0.872\n359\t      ],\n360\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage_ci\": [\n361\t        0.553,\n362\t        0.789\n363\t      ],\n364\t      \"qwq_temporal_coverage_ci\": [\n365\t        0.526,\n366\t        0.763\n367\t      ],\n368\t      \"r1_8b_delta_ci\": [\n369\t        0.198,\n370\t        0.412\n371\t      ],\n372\t      \"r1_32b_delta_ci\": [\n373\t        0.068,\n374\t        0.237\n375\t      ],\n376\t      \"qwq_delta_ci\": [\n377\t        0.089,\n378\t        0.251\n379\t      ],\n380\t      \"r1_32b_lead_time_ci\": [\n381\t        -1.47,\n382\t        2.51\n383\t      ],\n384\t      \"qwq_lead_time_ci\": [\n385\t        -2.83,\n386\t        1.38\n387\t      ],\n388\t      \"r1_8b_lead_time_ci\": [\n389\t        -2.91,\n390\t        0.73\n391\t      ],\n392\t      \"cross_transfer_llama_to_qwen_ci\": [\n393\t        0.289,\n394\t        0.553\n395\t      ],\n396\t      \"mahalanobis_r1_32b_ci\": [\n397\t        0.329,\n398\t        0.574\n399\t      ],\n400\t      \"n_resamples\": 1000,\n401\t      \"ci_method\": \"percentile bootstrap\"\n402\t    },\n403\t    \"tables\": [\n404\t      {\n405\t        \"name\": \"Table 8: Bootstrap 95% Confidence Intervals for Key Metrics\",\n406\t        \"columns\": [\n407\t          \"Metric\",\n408\t          \"r1-8b\",\n409\t          \"r1-32b\",\n410\t          \"QwQ-32B\"\n411\t        ],\n412\t        \"rows\": [\n413\t          [\n414\t            \"Static Coverage\",\n415\t            \"[0.326, 0.605]\",\n416\t            \"[0.395, 0.658]\",\n417\t            \"[0.351, 0.612]\"\n418\t          ],\n419\t          [\n420\t            \"Temporal W=15 Coverage\",\n421\t            \"[0.651, 0.872]\",\n422\t            \"[0.553, 0.789]\",\n423\t            \"[0.526, 0.763]\"\n424\t          ],\n425\t          [\n426\t            \"Delta (Temporal - Static)\",\n427\t            \"[0.198, 0.412]\",\n428\t            \"[0.068, 0.237]\",\n429\t            \"[0.089, 0.251]\"\n430\t          ],\n431\t          [\n432\t            \"Lead Time Mean\",\n433\t            \"[-2.91, 0.73]\",\n434\t            \"[-1.47, 2.51]\",\n435\t            \"[-2.83, 1.38]\"\n436\t          ],\n437\t          [\n438\t            \"Mahalanobis Coverage\",\n439\t            \"[0.186, 0.421]\",\n440\t            \"[0.329, 0.574]\",\n441\t            \"[0.284, 0.532]\"\n442\t          ]\n443\t        ]\n444\t      }\n445\t    ],\n446\t    \"figures\": [],\n447\t    \"analysis\": \"Bootstrap CIs confirm: (1) temporal advantage is statistically real on all architectures; (2) lead time is not distinguishable from zero on any model; (3) the attenuation on Qwen2.5 is real (non-overlapping CI centers) but not conclusively 'different' from Llama given sample sizes. This is methodologically honest — the paper can claim consistent improvement direction but must acknowledge power limitations. Recommend framing magnitude difference as 'observed' rather than 'statistically established' to avoid reviewer pushback on underpowered comparison.\\n\\nCIs are wide due to small test sets (38-43 traces) — this is expected and honest. Key implications: (1) The temporal advantage on Qwen2.5 is statistically significant (delta CI lower bound > 0 for both models), confirming the effect is real even if attenuated. (2) The r1-32b lead time CI spans [-1.47, 2.51], meaning the slightly positive mean (0.52) is NOT statistically distinguishable from zero — supporting 'near-zero' rather than 'positive' framing. (3) The delta CIs for Qwen2.5 [0.07, 0.24] vs Llama [0.20, 0.41] overlap at the boundary but are clearly shifted, supporting 'architecture-dependent magnitude' claim. (4) Mahalanobis CI on r1-32b spans 0.33-0.57, overlapping with static LR CI — the partial success claim needs careful hedging.\"\n448\t  },\n449\t  {\n450\t    \"name\": \"Window size ablation\",\n451\t    \"description\": \"Sweep W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all 3 models. Identify architecture-specific optimal window and diminishing returns curve.\",\n452\t    \"results\": {\n453\t      \"r1_8b_optimal_W\": 15,\n454\t      \"r1_8b_W_curve\": {\n455\t        \"1\": 0.465,\n456\t        \"3\": 0.535,\n457\t        \"5\": 0.651,\n458\t        \"10\": 0.698,\n459\t        \"15\": 0.767,\n460\t        \"20\": 0.744,\n461\t        \"25\": 0.721\n462\t      },\n463\t      \"r1_32b_optimal_W\": 10,\n464\t      \"r1_32b_W_curve\": {\n465\t        \"1\": 0.528,\n466\t        \"3\": 0.566,\n467\t        \"5\": 0.623,\n468\t        \"10\": 0.689,\n469\t        \"15\": 0.679,\n470\t        \"20\": 0.66,\n471\t        \"25\": 0.642\n472\t      },\n473\t      \"qwq_optimal_W\": 10,\n474\t      \"qwq_W_curve\": {\n475\t        \"1\": 0.482,\n476\t        \"3\": 0.519,\n477\t        \"5\": 0.593,\n478\t        \"10\": 0.667,\n479\t        \"15\": 0.648,\n480\t        \"20\": 0.63,\n481\t        \"25\": 0.611\n482\t      },\n483\t      \"r1_8b_saturation_W\": 15,\n484\t      \"qwen_saturation_W\": 10,\n485\t      \"overshoot_at_W25_qwen\": \"-3-4pp vs optimal\"\n486\t    },\n487\t    \"tables\": [\n488\t      {\n489\t        \"name\": \"Table 9: Window Size Ablation\",\n490\t        \"columns\": [\n491\t          \"W\",\n492\t          \"r1-8b Cov\",\n493\t          \"r1-32b Cov\",\n494\t          \"QwQ Cov\"\n495\t        ],\n496\t        \"rows\": [\n497\t          [\n498\t            1,\n499\t            0.465,\n500\t            0.528,\n501\t            0.482\n502\t          ],\n503\t          [\n504\t            3,\n505\t            0.535,\n506\t            0.566,\n507\t            0.519\n508\t          ],\n509\t          [\n510\t            5,\n511\t            0.651,\n512\t            0.623,\n513\t            0.593\n514\t          ],\n515\t          [\n516\t            10,\n517\t            0.698,\n518\t            0.689,\n519\t            0.667\n520\t          ],\n521\t          [\n522\t            15,\n523\t            0.767,\n524\t            0.679,\n525\t            0.648\n526\t          ],\n527\t          [\n528\t            20,\n529\t            0.744,\n530\t            0.66,\n531\t            0.63\n532\t          ],\n533\t          [\n534\t            25,\n535\t            0.721,\n536\t            0.642,\n537\t            0.611\n538\t          ]\n539\t        ]\n540\t      }\n541\t    ],\n542\t    \"figures\": [],\n543\t    \"analysis\": \"Window ablation is the strongest evidence for architecture-specific signal dynamics. The finding that Qwen2.5 peaks at W=10 (vs Llama at W=15) is non-trivial and provides both theoretical insight (signal concentration determines required temporal context) and practical guidance (don't blindly use the same W cross-architecture). This supports reframing the paper's contribution as 'characterization + practical method' — the characterization reveals WHY architectures differ and HOW to adapt.\\n\\nArchitecture-specific optimal windows emerge: Llama peaks at W=15, Qwen2.5 peaks at W=10. This reflects Qwen2.5's more concentrated signal geometry — shorter temporal context suffices because the safety-relevant information is more geometrically accessible per-step. Longer windows on Qwen2.5 actually HURT (overfitting to noise in the padded features) because the per-step signal is stronger, making the temporal redundancy in larger windows counterproductive. On Llama, the signal is more diffuse, requiring longer temporal context to accumulate enough evidence. This finding directly supports the 'architecture-specific optimal W' narrative and provides a practical recommendation for deployment (tune W per architecture). The diminishing returns curves have different shapes — Llama is concave with late saturation, Qwen2.5 saturates earlier and then declines.\"\n544\t  },\n545\t  {\n546\t    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation\",\n547\t    \"description\": \"Evaluate temporal probe at all 64 layers of r1-32b (sampling every 4th layer: L4,L8,...,L64). Confirm middle-layer optimality finding generalizes from r1-8b.\",\n548\t    \"results\": {\n549\t      \"best_layer_r1_32b\": \"L32\",\n550\t      \"best_coverage_L32\": 0.689,\n551\t      \"layer_coverage_curve\": {\n552\t        \"4\": 0.412,\n553\t        \"8\": 0.478,\n554\t        \"12\": 0.534,\n555\t        \"16\": 0.587,\n556\t        \"20\": 0.623,\n557\t        \"24\": 0.651,\n558\t        \"28\": 0.672,\n559\t        \"32\": 0.689,\n560\t        \"36\": 0.681,\n561\t        \"40\": 0.668,\n562\t        \"44\": 0.645,\n563\t        \"48\": 0.612,\n564\t        \"52\": 0.578,\n565\t        \"56\": 0.534,\n566\t        \"60\": 0.489,\n567\t        \"64\": 0.423\n568\t      },\n569\t      \"relative_best_position\": 0.5,\n570\t      \"r1_8b_relative_best_position\": 0.5,\n571\t      \"early_layer_coverage\": 0.412,\n572\t      \"final_layer_coverage\": 0.423\n573\t    },\n574\t    \"tables\": [\n575\t      {\n576\t        \"name\": \"Table 10: Layer Selection (r1-32b, W=10)\",\n577\t        \"columns\": [\n578\t          \"Layer\",\n579\t          \"Relative Depth\",\n580\t          \"Coverage\",\n581\t          \"Accuracy\"\n582\t        ],\n583\t        \"rows\": [\n584\t          [\n585\t            \"L8\",\n586\t            0.125,\n587\t            0.478,\n588\t            0.784\n589\t          ],\n590\t          [\n591\t            \"L16\",\n592\t            0.25,\n593\t            0.587,\n594\t            0.812\n595\t          ],\n596\t          [\n597\t            \"L24\",\n598\t            0.375,\n599\t            0.651,\n600\t            0.834\n601\t          ],\n602\t          [\n603\t            \"L32\",\n604\t            0.5,\n605\t            0.689,\n606\t            0.851\n607\t          ],\n608\t          [\n609\t            \"L40\",\n610\t            0.625,\n611\t            0.668,\n612\t            0.841\n613\t          ],\n614\t          [\n615\t            \"L48\",\n616\t            0.75,\n617\t            0.612,\n618\t            0.819\n619\t          ],\n620\t          [\n621\t            \"L56\",\n622\t            0.875,\n623\t            0.534,\n624\t            0.793\n625\t          ]\n626\t        ]\n627\t      }\n628\t    ],\n629\t    \"figures\": [],\n630\t    \"analysis\": \"Middle-layer optimality is a robust cross-architecture finding. The symmetric bell shape suggests safety state is encoded as a mid-level semantic abstraction, consistent across Llama and Qwen2.5. Practical implication: the 50% depth heuristic provides a reliable starting point for new architectures without expensive layer sweeps.\\n\\nMiddle-layer optimality generalizes: both architectures peak at the relative midpoint (L14/28 for r1-8b = 50%, L32/64 for r1-32b = 50%). This is expected from representation learning theory — middle layers encode semantic abstractions before task-specific decoding in later layers, and the safety reasoning state is a semantic-level concept. Early layers encode surface features (token identity), late layers encode output logits. The smooth bell curve with ~28pp range (41% to 69%) confirms layer choice matters and the finding is robust. This adds a practical recommendation: for new architectures, start at the 50% depth point.\"\n631\t  },\n632\t  {\n633\t    \"name\": \"16-label taxonomy supervision\",\n634\t    \"description\": \"Replace binary commitment labels with fine-grained 16-label taxonomy (Type 1-4 safety reasoning categories) for temporal probe training. Test whether richer supervision improves coverage or timing.\",\n635\t    \"results\": {\n636\t      \"r1_8b_16label_coverage\": 0.791,\n637\t      \"r1_8b_16label_lead_mean\": -0.82,\n638\t      \"r1_8b_16label_acc_macro\": 0.623,\n639\t      \"r1_8b_binary_from_16label_coverage\": 0.767,\n640\t      \"r1_32b_16label_coverage\": 0.704,\n641\t      \"r1_32b_16label_lead_mean\": 0.38,\n642\t      \"r1_32b_binary_from_16label_coverage\": 0.679,\n643\t      \"improvement_over_binary\": \"+2.4pp on r1-8b, +2.5pp on r1-32b\",\n644\t      \"label_confusion\": \"Type 2 (acknowledging harm) and Type 3 (refusing) most separable; Type 1 (reasoning about safety) confused with harmful reasoning\"\n645\t    },\n646\t    \"tables\": [\n647\t      {\n648\t        \"name\": \"Table 11: Fine-Grained Label Supervision\",\n649\t        \"columns\": [\n650\t          \"Model\",\n651\t          \"Supervision\",\n652\t          \"Coverage\",\n653\t          \"Lead Mean\",\n654\t          \"Macro Acc\"\n655\t        ],\n656\t        \"rows\": [\n657\t          [\n658\t            \"r1-8b\",\n659\t            \"Binary\",\n660\t            0.767,\n661\t            -1.09,\n662\t            0.848\n663\t          ],\n664\t          [\n665\t            \"r1-8b\",\n666\t            \"16-label\",\n667\t            0.791,\n668\t            -0.82,\n669\t            0.623\n670\t          ],\n671\t          [\n672\t            \"r1-32b\",\n673\t            \"Binary\",\n674\t            0.679,\n675\t            0.52,\n676\t            0.851\n677\t          ],\n678\t          [\n679\t            \"r1-32b\",\n680\t            \"16-label\",\n681\t            0.704,\n682\t            0.38,\n683\t            0.631\n684\t          ]\n685\t        ]\n686\t      }\n687\t    ],\n688\t    \"figures\": [],\n689\t    \"analysis\": \"Fine-grained labels offer marginal quantitative improvement but meaningful qualitative insight. The finding that Type 1 (safety reasoning) is confused with harmful reasoning in hidden-state space is theoretically interesting — it suggests the model uses similar computational circuits for both. This supports including the 16-label experiment in the paper for interpretability value, but it doesn't change the core quantitative story.\\n\\n16-label supervision provides marginal improvement (+2-3pp coverage) because the additional label structure helps the probe distinguish 'safety reasoning declining' from 'safety reasoning changing type.' However, the improvement is small because the binary commitment label already captures the key transition — the 16 categories provide redundant signal once the main safe→unsafe boundary is learned. Lead time doesn't meaningfully improve because the fundamental limitation (hidden states don't encode future trajectory) applies regardless of label granularity. The real value is interpretability: we can now characterize WHICH types of safety reasoning decline first (Type 1 reasoning-about-safety disappears before Type 3 refusal). This informs the Discussion but doesn't change core claims.\"\n690\t  },\n691\t  {\n692\t    \"name\": \"Oracle decomposition analysis\",\n693\t    \"description\": \"Analyze WHY the text-label oracle achieves +31 step lead time while hidden-state probes achieve ~0. Decompose oracle's advantage into specific label pattern features.\",\n694\t    \"results\": {\n695\t      \"oracle_key_features\": [\n696\t        \"consecutive_safe_count_decline\",\n697\t        \"safe_label_density_in_window\",\n698\t        \"longest_unsafe_streak\"\n699\t      ],\n700\t      \"feature_importance_ranking\": {\n701\t        \"consecutive_safe_decline\": 0.41,\n702\t        \"density_in_W10\": 0.28,\n703\t        \"longest_unsafe_streak\": 0.19,\n704\t        \"type_diversity_decline\": 0.12\n705\t      },\n706\t      \"oracle_ablation_no_future\": \"lead_mean drops from +31 to +2.1 when oracle only sees past labels\",\n707\t      \"oracle_ablation_current_only\": \"lead_mean = -0.5 (similar to probe)\",\n708\t      \"information_gap\": \"28.9 steps of lead come from FUTURE label visibility\",\n709\t      \"hidden_state_mutual_info_with_future_labels\": 0.043\n710\t    },\n711\t    \"tables\": [\n712\t      {\n713\t        \"name\": \"Table 12: Oracle Decomposition\",\n714\t        \"columns\": [\n715\t          \"Oracle Variant\",\n716\t          \"Lead Mean\",\n717\t          \"Coverage\",\n718\t          \"Information Source\"\n719\t        ],\n720\t        \"rows\": [\n721\t          [\n722\t            \"Full oracle (past+future)\",\n723\t            31,\n724\t            1.0,\n725\t            \"Complete label sequence\"\n726\t          ],\n727\t          [\n728\t            \"Past-only oracle\",\n729\t            2.1,\n730\t            0.82,\n731\t            \"Only labels at t≤current\"\n732\t          ],\n733\t          [\n734\t            \"Current-step oracle\",\n735\t            -0.5,\n736\t            0.73,\n737\t            \"Only label at t=current\"\n738\t          ],\n739\t          [\n740\t            \"Hidden-state probe (W=15)\",\n741\t            -1.09,\n742\t            0.767,\n743\t            \"Hidden states only\"\n744\t          ]\n745\t        ]\n746\t      }\n747\t    ],\n748\t    \"figures\": [],\n749\t    \"analysis\": \"The decomposition conclusively establishes WHY pre-textual detection fails: 93% of the oracle's lead comes from future information that hidden states cannot contain. This is a principled impossibility argument — not a method failure but a fundamental information-theoretic limitation. The past-only oracle's +2.1 steps suggests a small ceiling for any hidden-state approach that could additionally incorporate label history (i.e., hybrid text+hidden-state monitors). This motivates future work directions in the Discussion section.\\n\\nThe oracle's advantage is almost entirely from seeing FUTURE labels (+28.9 of +31 steps). When restricted to past-only labels, the oracle drops to +2.1 steps — only marginally better than hidden-state probes. This confirms the limitation is NOT that probes miss something in current hidden states, but that hidden states simply don't encode future safety trajectory. The mutual information between current hidden state and future labels (t+5 to t+15) is near-zero (0.043 bits), confirming there's no recoverable signal about the future. The implication: closing the gap requires either (a) future-label information (text monitoring), (b) multiple-step autoregressive hidden-state prediction, or (c) auxiliary training objectives that force the model to encode future safety trajectory.\"\n750\t  },\n751\t  {\n752\t    \"name\": \"OpenThinker-7B evaluation\",\n753\t    \"description\": \"4th model (Qwen2.5-7B base) to complete 2×2 architecture×scale matrix: {Llama-8B, Qwen2.5-7B} × {7-8B, 32B}. Controls for scale confound in cross-architecture comparison.\",\n754\t    \"results\": {\n755\t      \"openthinker_7b_static_coverage\": 0.512,\n756\t      \"openthinker_7b_temporal_w10_coverage\": 0.654,\n757\t      \"openthinker_7b_temporal_w15_coverage\": 0.641,\n758\t      \"openthinker_7b_delta\": 0.142,\n759\t      \"openthinker_7b_lead_mean\": -0.91,\n760\t      \"openthinker_7b_fisher_orig\": 10.12,\n761\t      \"openthinker_7b_fisher_pca3\": 2.37,\n762\t      \"openthinker_7b_optimal_W\": 10,\n763\t      \"scale_effect_llama\": \"8B→32B not applicable (different base arch)\",\n764\t      \"scale_effect_qwen\": \"7B vs 32B — coverage +1.6pp static, +3.5pp temporal (minimal scale effect)\",\n765\t      \"architecture_effect_at_7_8B\": \"Llama-8B vs Qwen2.5-7B: -4.7pp static, +12.6pp temporal delta difference\"\n766\t    },\n767\t    \"tables\": [\n768\t      {\n769\t        \"name\": \"Table 13: 2×2 Architecture × Scale Matrix\",\n770\t        \"columns\": [\n771\t          \"Model\",\n772\t          \"Architecture\",\n773\t          \"Scale\",\n774\t          \"Static Cov\",\n775\t          \"Temporal Cov\",\n776\t          \"Delta\",\n777\t          \"Optimal W\"\n778\t        ],\n779\t        \"rows\": [\n780\t          [\n781\t            \"r1-8b\",\n782\t            \"Llama-3.1\",\n783\t            \"8B\",\n784\t            0.465,\n785\t            0.767,\n786\t            \"+30.2pp\",\n787\t            15\n788\t          ],\n789\t          [\n790\t            \"OpenThinker-7B\",\n791\t            \"Qwen2.5\",\n792\t            \"7B\",\n793\t            0.512,\n794\t            0.654,\n795\t            \"+14.2pp\",\n796\t            10\n797\t          ],\n798\t          [\n799\t            \"r1-32b\",\n800\t            \"Qwen2.5\",\n801\t            \"32B\",\n802\t            0.528,\n803\t            0.689,\n804\t            \"+16.1pp\",\n805\t            10\n806\t          ],\n807\t          [\n808\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n809\t            \"Qwen2.5\",\n810\t            \"32B\",\n811\t            0.482,\n812\t            0.648,\n813\t            \"+16.6pp\",\n814\t            10\n815\t          ]\n816\t        ]\n817\t      }\n818\t    ],\n819\t    \"figures\": [],\n820\t    \"analysis\": \"The 2×2 matrix is clean: architecture determines temporal advantage magnitude (30pp Llama vs 14-17pp Qwen2.5), while scale has minimal effect within architecture. This eliminates a key confound and strengthens the characterization story. The paper can now claim that the temporal advantage difference is an architecture-level property, likely related to how safety signal is geometrically organized in the representation space.\\n\\nOpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base) confirms the architecture effect is NOT a scale confound. At matched scale (7-8B), the same pattern holds: Qwen2.5 has higher static coverage (51% vs 47%) but lower temporal delta (14pp vs 30pp). The optimal window is W=10 (same as Qwen2.5-32B), further confirming this is an architecture-level property. Scale effect within Qwen2.5 is minimal (+1.6pp static, +3.5pp temporal from 7B→32B), ruling out the hypothesis that 'Qwen2.5 only attenuates because 32B models have different dynamics.' The 2×2 matrix cleanly separates architecture effect (dominant) from scale effect (minimal), strengthening the architectural analysis contribution.\"\n821\t  },\n822\t  {\n823\t    \"name\": \"GRU/Attention probe upper bound on 32B models\",\n824\t    \"description\": \"Train GRU (hidden=256) and self-attention probes on r1-32b and QwQ-32B to verify whether complex architectures offer improvement over temporal LR on Qwen2.5 base models.\",\n825\t    \"results\": {\n826\t      \"r1_32b_gru_coverage\": 0.698,\n827\t      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 0.89,\n828\t      \"r1_32b_gru_acc\": 0.847,\n829\t      \"r1_32b_attention_coverage\": 0.685,\n830\t      \"r1_32b_attention_lead_mean\": 0.31,\n831\t      \"qwq_gru_coverage\": 0.667,\n832\t      \"qwq_gru_lead_mean\": -0.42,\n833\t      \"qwq_attention_coverage\": 0.654,\n834\t      \"qwq_attention_lead_mean\": -0.58,\n835\t      \"gru_vs_lr_delta_r1_32b\": \"+1.9pp coverage\",\n836\t      \"gru_vs_lr_delta_qwq\": \"+1.9pp coverage\",\n837\t      \"gru_params\": 1622913,\n838\t      \"attention_params\": 2105344,\n839\t      \"lr_temporal_params\": 77025\n840\t    },\n841\t    \"tables\": [\n842\t      {\n843\t        \"name\": \"Table 14: Complex Probe Architectures on Qwen2.5 Models\",\n844\t        \"columns\": [\n845\t          \"Model\",\n846\t          \"Method\",\n847\t          \"Params\",\n848\t          \"Coverage\",\n849\t          \"Lead Mean\",\n850\t          \"vs Temporal LR\"\n851\t        ],\n852\t        \"rows\": [\n853\t          [\n854\t            \"r1-32b\",\n855\t            \"Temporal LR (W=10)\",\n856\t            \"77K\",\n857\t            0.689,\n858\t            0.52,\n859\t            \"baseline\"\n860\t          ],\n861\t          [\n862\t            \"r1-32b\",\n863\t            \"GRU-256\",\n864\t            \"1.6M\",\n865\t            0.698,\n866\t            0.89,\n867\t            \"+0.9pp\"\n868\t          ],\n869\t          [\n870\t            \"r1-32b\",\n871\t            \"Self-Attention\",\n872\t            \"2.1M\",\n873\t            0.685,\n874\t            0.31,\n875\t            \"-0.4pp\"\n876\t          ],\n877\t          [\n878\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n879\t            \"Temporal LR (W=10)\",\n880\t            \"77K\",\n881\t            0.667,\n882\t            -0.73,\n883\t            \"baseline\"\n884\t          ],\n885\t          [\n886\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n887\t            \"GRU-256\",\n888\t            \"1.6M\",\n889\t            0.667,\n890\t            -0.42,\n891\t            \"+0.0pp\"\n892\t          ],\n893\t          [\n894\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n895\t            \"Self-Attention\",\n896\t            \"2.1M\",\n897\t            0.654,\n898\t            -0.58,\n899\t            \"-1.3pp\"\n900\t          ]\n901\t        ]\n902\t      }\n903\t    ],\n904\t    \"figures\": [],\n905\t    \"analysis\": \"Complex probes don't help on Qwen2.5, confirming the 'simplicity is optimal' finding generalizes. The slightly larger GRU improvement on r1-32b (+0.9pp coverage, +0.37 lead) compared to QwQ (+0.0pp, +0.31 lead) is within noise. Key takeaway: the 30pp temporal advantage ceiling on Llama and 15-17pp ceiling on Qwen2.5 appear to be INFORMATION limits, not model capacity limits — better probes can't extract signal that isn't there.\\n\\nGRU provides marginal improvement (+1.9pp) over temporal LR on Qwen2.5, consistent with the r1-8b finding that complex architectures don't substantially help. The improvement is smaller than on Llama (where GRU gained ~3.7pp) because Qwen2.5's more concentrated signal is already well-captured by linear methods — the nonlinear capacity of GRU has less to exploit. Self-attention probe performs slightly WORSE than GRU (expected at this data scale — attention is data-hungry). The 'simplicity is optimal at current data scales' conclusion holds cross-architecture. The 20× parameter count of GRU (1.6M vs 77K) for <2pp improvement confirms the cost-benefit analysis favors temporal LR for practical deployment.\"\n906\t  }\n907\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c514e65-084f-43fa-8596-877e1ee4e682","uuid":"43217b57-e754-4d5f-8fdf-446c609c0a76","timestamp":"2026-05-16T20:02:52.935Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T194646/median/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture static probe evaluation\",\n    \"description\": \"Train static LogisticRegression on hidden states from DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B at layer 20 (proportional mid-layer for 64-layer model). Evaluate coverage (crossing rate), accuracy, and Fisher discriminant ratio in original and PCA-reduced spaces.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_static_acc\": 0.8341,\n      \"r1_32b_static_coverage\": 0.5283,\n      \"r1_32b_static_lead_mean\": -1.4,\n      \"r1_32b_fisher_orig_L32\": 11.23,\n      \"r1_32b_fisher_orig_L40\": 12.07,\n      \"r1_32b_fisher_pca3_L32\": 2.61,\n      \"r1_32b_fisher_pca3_L40\": 2.84,\n      \"r1_32b_var_explained_3pc\": 0.231,\n      \"qwq_32b_static_acc\": 0.8189,\n      \"qwq_32b_static_coverage\": 0.4815,\n      \"qwq_32b_static_lead_mean\": -1.8,\n      \"qwq_32b_fisher_orig_L32\": 9.74,\n      \"qwq_32b_fisher_orig_L40\": 10.52,\n      \"qwq_32b_fisher_pca3_L32\": 2.19,\n      \"qwq_32b_fisher_pca3_L40\": 2.43,\n      \"qwq_32b_var_explained_3pc\": 0.214,\n      \"num_traces_r1_32b\": 187,\n      \"num_traces_qwq\": 192,\n      \"split_ratio\": \"112/37/38 and 115/38/39\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Static Probe Results Across Architectures\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Base Arch\",\n          \"Hidden Dim\",\n          \"Best Layer\",\n          \"Accuracy\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"Lead Time\",\n          \"Fisher_orig\",\n          \"Fisher_PCA3\",\n          \"Var_3PC\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"Llama-3.1\",\n            4096,\n            \"L14\",\n            0.817,\n            0.465,\n            -2.0,\n            17.88,\n            0.93,\n            0.166\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"Qwen2.5\",\n            5120,\n            \"L32\",\n            0.834,\n            0.528,\n            -1.4,\n            12.07,\n            2.84,\n            0.231\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"Qwen2.5\",\n            5120,\n            \"L32\",\n            0.819,\n            0.482,\n            -1.8,\n            10.52,\n            2.43,\n            0.214\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Claim 1 validated cross-architecture: static probes achieve 48-53% coverage on Qwen2.5 models, remaining below 60% threshold. Architectural difference in signal distribution is quantifiable — Qwen2.5 has lower full-space Fisher (signal more diffuse per-dimension) but higher PCA-space Fisher (signal more concentrated in leading components). This sets up the key cross-architecture comparison: Qwen2.5's different signal geometry may alter temporal probe dynamics.\\n\\nQwen2.5 architecture distributes safety signal differently than Llama — Fisher discriminant in original space is 9-12 (vs Llama's 17-18) because Qwen2.5's wider MLP and GQA attention spread safety-relevant features across more dimensions with lower per-dimension separability. However, PCA top-3 captures more variance (21-23% vs Llama's 16.6%) and Fisher in PCA space is higher (2.2-2.8 vs Llama's ~1), indicating Qwen2.5 concentrates safety signal slightly MORE in leading PCs. Static coverage is marginally higher than Llama (48-53% vs 46.5%) because this higher PCA concentration gives a linear probe slightly more accessible signal, but still below 60% — confirming Claim 1 generalizes. The lower full-space Fisher explains why the absolute coverage remains limited despite better geometric accessibility.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation\",\n    \"description\": \"Train temporal window LogisticRegression with W=15 on r1-32b and QwQ-32B hidden states. Compare coverage improvement (delta over static) to r1-8b's +30pp.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_acc\": 0.8512,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_coverage\": 0.6792,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean\": 0.52,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_lead_median\": 0,\n      \"r1_32b_delta_over_static\": 0.1509,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_acc\": 0.8421,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_coverage\": 0.6481,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean\": -0.73,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_lead_median\": -1,\n      \"qwq_32b_delta_over_static\": 0.1666,\n      \"r1_8b_temporal_w15_coverage_ref\": 0.7674,\n      \"r1_8b_delta_ref\": 0.3023,\n      \"attenuation_factor_r1_32b\": 0.5,\n      \"attenuation_factor_qwq\": 0.55\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: Temporal Window Probe (W=15) Across Architectures\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Static Cov\",\n          \"Temporal Cov\",\n          \"Delta\",\n          \"Lead Mean\",\n          \"Lead Median\",\n          \"Acc\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n            0.465,\n            0.767,\n            \"+30.2pp\",\n            -1.09,\n            -1,\n            0.848\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\",\n            0.528,\n            0.679,\n            \"+15.1pp\",\n            0.52,\n            0,\n            0.851\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\",\n            0.482,\n            0.648,\n            \"+16.7pp\",\n            -0.73,\n            -1,\n            0.842\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Temporal advantage is real and consistent (+15-17pp) but architecture-dependent in magnitude. The 30pp gain on Llama attenuates to ~16pp on Qwen2.5, likely because Qwen2.5's signal geometry (higher PCA concentration) gives static probes a head start. Claim 2 must be softened: temporal probes 'consistently improve coverage with architecture-dependent magnitude' rather than 'saturate oracle ceiling cross-architecture.' The r1-32b slightly positive lead time (0.52 steps) is intriguing but not statistically robust — this becomes the directional sub-finding that motivates future work.\\n\\nTemporal advantage exists but is attenuated to ~15-17pp (vs 30pp on Llama). The attenuation occurs because Qwen2.5's higher PCA concentration means static probes already capture more of the accessible signal — there's less 'headroom' for temporal context to add. The temporal probe still helps because safety reasoning density decline patterns exist cross-architecture, but the already-higher static baseline leaves less room for improvement. Coverage reaches 65-68% rather than 77% — this does NOT saturate the oracle ceiling (which would be ~77% if oracle patterns are similar). The r1-32b result of +0.52 lead time is the 'ambiguous' sub-finding: slightly positive but within noise given bootstrap CI will span [-1.5, 2.5]. QwQ remains clearly negative. This architecture-dependent magnitude is the key finding that reshapes Claim 2.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-model transfer evaluation\",\n    \"description\": \"Zero-shot cross-architecture transfer: train probe on r1-8b (Llama, dim=4096), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5, dim=5120) via learned linear projection. And reverse direction. Evaluate with both static and temporal probes.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"llama_to_qwen_static_coverage\": 0.372,\n      \"llama_to_qwen_temporal_coverage\": 0.426,\n      \"qwen_to_llama_static_coverage\": 0.349,\n      \"qwen_to_llama_temporal_coverage\": 0.401,\n      \"within_arch_qwen_to_qwen_static\": 0.491,\n      \"within_arch_qwen_to_qwen_temporal\": 0.612,\n      \"projection_dim\": 512,\n      \"projection_method\": \"learned linear (trained on 50 aligned traces)\",\n      \"random_baseline_coverage\": 0.167\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: Cross-Architecture Transfer Results\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Train Model\",\n          \"Test Model\",\n          \"Probe Type\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"vs In-Domain\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n            \"Static\",\n            0.372,\n            \"0.528 (-15.6pp)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n            \"Temporal W=15\",\n            0.426,\n            \"0.679 (-25.3pp)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n            \"Static\",\n            0.349,\n            \"0.465 (-11.6pp)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n            \"Temporal W=15\",\n            0.401,\n            \"0.767 (-36.6pp)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n            \"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\",\n            \"Static\",\n            0.491,\n            \"0.482 (+0.9pp)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b (Qwen)\",\n            \"QwQ-32B (Qwen)\",\n            \"Temporal W=15\",\n            0.612,\n            \"0.648 (-3.6pp)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cross-architecture transfer confirms the 'architecture-specific signal geometry' hypothesis. Within-architecture transfer nearly matches in-domain performance (especially Qwen→Qwen), while cross-architecture transfer drops 12-37pp. This supports framing cross-architecture validation as characterization rather than claiming a universal detector — practical deployment requires per-architecture probe training. The finding strengthens the architectural analysis contribution axis.\\n\\nCross-architecture zero-shot transfer is weak (35-43% coverage) because the safety signal occupies architecture-specific subspaces — a linear projection trained on few aligned samples cannot fully recover the mapping between Llama and Qwen2.5 hidden state geometries. The projection captures gross safe/unsafe distinctions (well above random baseline of 16.7%) but loses the fine-grained temporal patterns that drive the +15-30pp temporal advantage. Within-architecture transfer (Qwen→Qwen, r1-32b→QwQ) performs substantially better (49-61%) because both Qwen2.5 models share similar signal geometry. This confirms the hypothesis that cross-architecture transfer requires architecture-specific fine-tuning — the safety concept is universal but its geometric instantiation is architecture-dependent.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Lead time analysis across models\",\n    \"description\": \"Analyze lead time distributions for temporal probes on all 3 models. Report per-model distributions, positive lead rate, and compare to oracle's +31 step advantage.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_lead_mean\": -1.09,\n      \"r1_8b_lead_median\": -1,\n      \"r1_8b_positive_lead_rate\": 0.302,\n      \"r1_32b_lead_mean\": 0.52,\n      \"r1_32b_lead_median\": 0,\n      \"r1_32b_positive_lead_rate\": 0.421,\n      \"qwq_lead_mean\": -0.73,\n      \"qwq_lead_median\": -1,\n      \"qwq_positive_lead_rate\": 0.333,\n      \"oracle_lead_mean_all_models\": 31,\n      \"gap_probe_vs_oracle\": \"29-32 steps across models\",\n      \"r1_32b_lead_std\": 4.8,\n      \"qwq_lead_std\": 5.1,\n      \"r1_32b_lead_p25\": -2,\n      \"r1_32b_lead_p75\": 3\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: Lead Time Distribution by Model\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Mean\",\n          \"Median\",\n          \"Std\",\n          \"P25\",\n          \"P75\",\n          \"% Positive\",\n          \"Oracle Mean\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            -1.09,\n            -1,\n            4.2,\n            -4,\n            1,\n            \"30.2%\",\n            31\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            0.52,\n            0,\n            4.8,\n            -2,\n            3,\n            \"42.1%\",\n            31\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            -0.73,\n            -1,\n            5.1,\n            -3,\n            2,\n            \"33.3%\",\n            31\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The near-zero lead time generalizes cross-architecture. The r1-32b slightly positive mean is a directional curiosity — 42% of traces show positive lead (vs 30% on Llama) but the absolute values remain too small for practical early warning. The 30-step gap between probe and oracle persists uniformly, confirming hidden states encode CURRENT safety state but not FUTURE trajectory. Claim 3 reframed as 'lead time is near-zero across architectures' — a softer statement that accommodates the r1-32b sub-finding while maintaining the core limitation message.\\n\\nLead time remains near-zero across all architectures, confirming the fundamental limitation of hidden-state approaches. The r1-32b result (+0.52 mean) is the one 'ambiguous' sub-finding — technically positive but the distribution spans [-8, +12] with median at 0, so it's not practically meaningful. The slight positive tendency on r1-32b may reflect Qwen2.5's more concentrated signal geometry making the commitment transition slightly more abrupt (and thus detectable 1-2 steps earlier in some traces). However, this does NOT constitute reliable pre-textual detection — the oracle still achieves +31 steps via label-sequence patterns invisible to hidden states. Claim 3 generalizes but should be stated as 'near-zero' rather than 'strictly negative' to accommodate the r1-32b directional finding.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline\",\n    \"description\": \"Implement Mahalanobis distance detector using safe-trace covariance estimation. Compute per-step MD, threshold for anomaly detection on all 3 models. Expected to partially work on Qwen2.5 (higher PCA concentration) but fail on Llama.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_mahalanobis_coverage\": 0.302,\n      \"r1_8b_mahalanobis_acc\": 0.714,\n      \"r1_8b_mahalanobis_auroc\": 0.683,\n      \"r1_32b_mahalanobis_coverage\": 0.453,\n      \"r1_32b_mahalanobis_acc\": 0.779,\n      \"r1_32b_mahalanobis_auroc\": 0.752,\n      \"qwq_mahalanobis_coverage\": 0.407,\n      \"qwq_mahalanobis_acc\": 0.761,\n      \"qwq_mahalanobis_auroc\": 0.734,\n      \"num_safe_traces_covariance\": \"70/65/68 per model\",\n      \"threshold_method\": \"95th percentile of safe-trace MD distribution\",\n      \"pca_reduced_md_r1_8b\": 0.256,\n      \"pca_reduced_md_r1_32b\": 0.372\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: Mahalanobis Distance Baseline\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"Accuracy\",\n          \"AUROC\",\n          \"vs Static LR\",\n          \"vs Temporal LR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b (Llama)\",\n            0.302,\n            0.714,\n            0.683,\n            \"-16.3pp\",\n            \"-46.5pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\",\n            0.453,\n            0.779,\n            0.752,\n            \"-7.5pp\",\n            \"-22.6pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\",\n            0.407,\n            0.761,\n            0.734,\n            \"-7.5pp\",\n            \"-24.1pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Mahalanobis partially works on Qwen2.5 (40-45% coverage, AUROC 0.73-0.75) — this is the 'ambiguous sub-finding' of the median tier. The result is architecturally explained by signal concentration in leading PCs, which means TrajGuard-style detectors would have partial success on Qwen2.5 reasoning models but near-complete failure on Llama-based ones. Reframe Claim 1 to note that 'geometric detectors show architecture-dependent effectiveness correlated with PCA concentration of safety signal' rather than blanket failure. Temporal probes still dominate in all cases, preserving the core story.\\n\\nMahalanobis distance PARTIALLY works on Qwen2.5 (40-45% coverage) but fails on Llama (30%). This is directly explained by the PCA concentration difference: Qwen2.5's higher Fisher in PCA space (2.4-2.8 vs Llama's ~1) means the covariance-based geometric detector can capture more of the safety signal, since more signal lives in the directions that dominate the covariance matrix. On Llama, safety signal is distributed across minor PCs that the covariance matrix effectively ignores — Mahalanobis detects gross distribution shifts but misses the subtle safety-specific directions. This is the 'ambiguous' result for this experiment: Mahalanobis doesn't completely fail on Qwen2.5 (as we'd predicted from Llama-only results), complicating the 'existing detectors fail' narrative. Must reframe as 'architecture-dependent failure mode' rather than universal failure.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Bootstrap confidence intervals\",\n    \"description\": \"1000 bootstrap resamples on all key metrics across all models and methods. Report 95% CIs to support statistical claims.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_temporal_coverage_ci\": [\n        0.651,\n        0.872\n      ],\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage_ci\": [\n        0.553,\n        0.789\n      ],\n      \"qwq_temporal_coverage_ci\": [\n        0.526,\n        0.763\n      ],\n      \"r1_8b_delta_ci\": [\n        0.198,\n        0.412\n      ],\n      \"r1_32b_delta_ci\": [\n        0.068,\n        0.237\n      ],\n      \"qwq_delta_ci\": [\n        0.089,\n        0.251\n      ],\n      \"r1_32b_lead_time_ci\": [\n        -1.47,\n        2.51\n      ],\n      \"qwq_lead_time_ci\": [\n        -2.83,\n        1.38\n      ],\n      \"r1_8b_lead_time_ci\": [\n        -2.91,\n        0.73\n      ],\n      \"cross_transfer_llama_to_qwen_ci\": [\n        0.289,\n        0.553\n      ],\n      \"mahalanobis_r1_32b_ci\": [\n        0.329,\n        0.574\n      ],\n      \"n_resamples\": 1000,\n      \"ci_method\": \"percentile bootstrap\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8: Bootstrap 95% Confidence Intervals for Key Metrics\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"r1-8b\",\n          \"r1-32b\",\n          \"QwQ-32B\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Static Coverage\",\n            \"[0.326, 0.605]\",\n            \"[0.395, 0.658]\",\n            \"[0.351, 0.612]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Temporal W=15 Coverage\",\n            \"[0.651, 0.872]\",\n            \"[0.553, 0.789]\",\n            \"[0.526, 0.763]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Delta (Temporal - Static)\",\n            \"[0.198, 0.412]\",\n            \"[0.068, 0.237]\",\n            \"[0.089, 0.251]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Lead Time Mean\",\n            \"[-2.91, 0.73]\",\n            \"[-1.47, 2.51]\",\n            \"[-2.83, 1.38]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Mahalanobis Coverage\",\n            \"[0.186, 0.421]\",\n            \"[0.329, 0.574]\",\n            \"[0.284, 0.532]\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Bootstrap CIs confirm: (1) temporal advantage is statistically real on all architectures; (2) lead time is not distinguishable from zero on any model; (3) the attenuation on Qwen2.5 is real (non-overlapping CI centers) but not conclusively 'different' from Llama given sample sizes. This is methodologically honest — the paper can claim consistent improvement direction but must acknowledge power limitations. Recommend framing magnitude difference as 'observed' rather than 'statistically established' to avoid reviewer pushback on underpowered comparison.\\n\\nCIs are wide due to small test sets (38-43 traces) — this is expected and honest. Key implications: (1) The temporal advantage on Qwen2.5 is statistically significant (delta CI lower bound > 0 for both models), confirming the effect is real even if attenuated. (2) The r1-32b lead time CI spans [-1.47, 2.51], meaning the slightly positive mean (0.52) is NOT statistically distinguishable from zero — supporting 'near-zero' rather than 'positive' framing. (3) The delta CIs for Qwen2.5 [0.07, 0.24] vs Llama [0.20, 0.41] overlap at the boundary but are clearly shifted, supporting 'architecture-dependent magnitude' claim. (4) Mahalanobis CI on r1-32b spans 0.33-0.57, overlapping with static LR CI — the partial success claim needs careful hedging.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Window size ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"Sweep W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25 on all 3 models. Identify architecture-specific optimal window and diminishing returns curve.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_optimal_W\": 15,\n      \"r1_8b_W_curve\": {\n        \"1\": 0.465,\n        \"3\": 0.535,\n        \"5\": 0.651,\n        \"10\": 0.698,\n        \"15\": 0.767,\n        \"20\": 0.744,\n        \"25\": 0.721\n      },\n      \"r1_32b_optimal_W\": 10,\n      \"r1_32b_W_curve\": {\n        \"1\": 0.528,\n        \"3\": 0.566,\n        \"5\": 0.623,\n        \"10\": 0.689,\n        \"15\": 0.679,\n        \"20\": 0.66,\n        \"25\": 0.642\n      },\n      \"qwq_optimal_W\": 10,\n      \"qwq_W_curve\": {\n        \"1\": 0.482,\n        \"3\": 0.519,\n        \"5\": 0.593,\n        \"10\": 0.667,\n        \"15\": 0.648,\n        \"20\": 0.63,\n        \"25\": 0.611\n      },\n      \"r1_8b_saturation_W\": 15,\n      \"qwen_saturation_W\": 10,\n      \"overshoot_at_W25_qwen\": \"-3-4pp vs optimal\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 9: Window Size Ablation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"W\",\n          \"r1-8b Cov\",\n          \"r1-32b Cov\",\n          \"QwQ Cov\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            0.465,\n            0.528,\n            0.482\n          ],\n          [\n            3,\n            0.535,\n            0.566,\n            0.519\n          ],\n          [\n            5,\n            0.651,\n            0.623,\n            0.593\n          ],\n          [\n            10,\n            0.698,\n            0.689,\n            0.667\n          ],\n          [\n            15,\n            0.767,\n            0.679,\n            0.648\n          ],\n          [\n            20,\n            0.744,\n            0.66,\n            0.63\n          ],\n          [\n            25,\n            0.721,\n            0.642,\n            0.611\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Window ablation is the strongest evidence for architecture-specific signal dynamics. The finding that Qwen2.5 peaks at W=10 (vs Llama at W=15) is non-trivial and provides both theoretical insight (signal concentration determines required temporal context) and practical guidance (don't blindly use the same W cross-architecture). This supports reframing the paper's contribution as 'characterization + practical method' — the characterization reveals WHY architectures differ and HOW to adapt.\\n\\nArchitecture-specific optimal windows emerge: Llama peaks at W=15, Qwen2.5 peaks at W=10. This reflects Qwen2.5's more concentrated signal geometry — shorter temporal context suffices because the safety-relevant information is more geometrically accessible per-step. Longer windows on Qwen2.5 actually HURT (overfitting to noise in the padded features) because the per-step signal is stronger, making the temporal redundancy in larger windows counterproductive. On Llama, the signal is more diffuse, requiring longer temporal context to accumulate enough evidence. This finding directly supports the 'architecture-specific optimal W' narrative and provides a practical recommendation for deployment (tune W per architecture). The diminishing returns curves have different shapes — Llama is concave with late saturation, Qwen2.5 saturates earlier and then declines.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"Evaluate temporal probe at all 64 layers of r1-32b (sampling every 4th layer: L4,L8,...,L64). Confirm middle-layer optimality finding generalizes from r1-8b.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"best_layer_r1_32b\": \"L32\",\n      \"best_coverage_L32\": 0.689,\n      \"layer_coverage_curve\": {\n        \"4\": 0.412,\n        \"8\": 0.478,\n        \"12\": 0.534,\n        \"16\": 0.587,\n        \"20\": 0.623,\n        \"24\": 0.651,\n        \"28\": 0.672,\n        \"32\": 0.689,\n        \"36\": 0.681,\n        \"40\": 0.668,\n        \"44\": 0.645,\n        \"48\": 0.612,\n        \"52\": 0.578,\n        \"56\": 0.534,\n        \"60\": 0.489,\n        \"64\": 0.423\n      },\n      \"relative_best_position\": 0.5,\n      \"r1_8b_relative_best_position\": 0.5,\n      \"early_layer_coverage\": 0.412,\n      \"final_layer_coverage\": 0.423\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 10: Layer Selection (r1-32b, W=10)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Layer\",\n          \"Relative Depth\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"Accuracy\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"L8\",\n            0.125,\n            0.478,\n            0.784\n          ],\n          [\n            \"L16\",\n            0.25,\n            0.587,\n            0.812\n          ],\n          [\n            \"L24\",\n            0.375,\n            0.651,\n            0.834\n          ],\n          [\n            \"L32\",\n            0.5,\n            0.689,\n            0.851\n          ],\n          [\n            \"L40\",\n            0.625,\n            0.668,\n            0.841\n          ],\n          [\n            \"L48\",\n            0.75,\n            0.612,\n            0.819\n          ],\n          [\n            \"L56\",\n            0.875,\n            0.534,\n            0.793\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Middle-layer optimality is a robust cross-architecture finding. The symmetric bell shape suggests safety state is encoded as a mid-level semantic abstraction, consistent across Llama and Qwen2.5. Practical implication: the 50% depth heuristic provides a reliable starting point for new architectures without expensive layer sweeps.\\n\\nMiddle-layer optimality generalizes: both architectures peak at the relative midpoint (L14/28 for r1-8b = 50%, L32/64 for r1-32b = 50%). This is expected from representation learning theory — middle layers encode semantic abstractions before task-specific decoding in later layers, and the safety reasoning state is a semantic-level concept. Early layers encode surface features (token identity), late layers encode output logits. The smooth bell curve with ~28pp range (41% to 69%) confirms layer choice matters and the finding is robust. This adds a practical recommendation: for new architectures, start at the 50% depth point.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"16-label taxonomy supervision\",\n    \"description\": \"Replace binary commitment labels with fine-grained 16-label taxonomy (Type 1-4 safety reasoning categories) for temporal probe training. Test whether richer supervision improves coverage or timing.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_16label_coverage\": 0.791,\n      \"r1_8b_16label_lead_mean\": -0.82,\n      \"r1_8b_16label_acc_macro\": 0.623,\n      \"r1_8b_binary_from_16label_coverage\": 0.767,\n      \"r1_32b_16label_coverage\": 0.704,\n      \"r1_32b_16label_lead_mean\": 0.38,\n      \"r1_32b_binary_from_16label_coverage\": 0.679,\n      \"improvement_over_binary\": \"+2.4pp on r1-8b, +2.5pp on r1-32b\",\n      \"label_confusion\": \"Type 2 (acknowledging harm) and Type 3 (refusing) most separable; Type 1 (reasoning about safety) confused with harmful reasoning\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 11: Fine-Grained Label Supervision\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Supervision\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"Lead Mean\",\n          \"Macro Acc\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"Binary\",\n            0.767,\n            -1.09,\n            0.848\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"16-label\",\n            0.791,\n            -0.82,\n            0.623\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"Binary\",\n            0.679,\n            0.52,\n            0.851\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"16-label\",\n            0.704,\n            0.38,\n            0.631\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Fine-grained labels offer marginal quantitative improvement but meaningful qualitative insight. The finding that Type 1 (safety reasoning) is confused with harmful reasoning in hidden-state space is theoretically interesting — it suggests the model uses similar computational circuits for both. This supports including the 16-label experiment in the paper for interpretability value, but it doesn't change the core quantitative story.\\n\\n16-label supervision provides marginal improvement (+2-3pp coverage) because the additional label structure helps the probe distinguish 'safety reasoning declining' from 'safety reasoning changing type.' However, the improvement is small because the binary commitment label already captures the key transition — the 16 categories provide redundant signal once the main safe→unsafe boundary is learned. Lead time doesn't meaningfully improve because the fundamental limitation (hidden states don't encode future trajectory) applies regardless of label granularity. The real value is interpretability: we can now characterize WHICH types of safety reasoning decline first (Type 1 reasoning-about-safety disappears before Type 3 refusal). This informs the Discussion but doesn't change core claims.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Oracle decomposition analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Analyze WHY the text-label oracle achieves +31 step lead time while hidden-state probes achieve ~0. Decompose oracle's advantage into specific label pattern features.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"oracle_key_features\": [\n        \"consecutive_safe_count_decline\",\n        \"safe_label_density_in_window\",\n        \"longest_unsafe_streak\"\n      ],\n      \"feature_importance_ranking\": {\n        \"consecutive_safe_decline\": 0.41,\n        \"density_in_W10\": 0.28,\n        \"longest_unsafe_streak\": 0.19,\n        \"type_diversity_decline\": 0.12\n      },\n      \"oracle_ablation_no_future\": \"lead_mean drops from +31 to +2.1 when oracle only sees past labels\",\n      \"oracle_ablation_current_only\": \"lead_mean = -0.5 (similar to probe)\",\n      \"information_gap\": \"28.9 steps of lead come from FUTURE label visibility\",\n      \"hidden_state_mutual_info_with_future_labels\": 0.043\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 12: Oracle Decomposition\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Oracle Variant\",\n          \"Lead Mean\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"Information Source\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Full oracle (past+future)\",\n            31,\n            1.0,\n            \"Complete label sequence\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Past-only oracle\",\n            2.1,\n            0.82,\n            \"Only labels at t≤current\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Current-step oracle\",\n            -0.5,\n            0.73,\n            \"Only label at t=current\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Hidden-state probe (W=15)\",\n            -1.09,\n            0.767,\n            \"Hidden states only\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The decomposition conclusively establishes WHY pre-textual detection fails: 93% of the oracle's lead comes from future information that hidden states cannot contain. This is a principled impossibility argument — not a method failure but a fundamental information-theoretic limitation. The past-only oracle's +2.1 steps suggests a small ceiling for any hidden-state approach that could additionally incorporate label history (i.e., hybrid text+hidden-state monitors). This motivates future work directions in the Discussion section.\\n\\nThe oracle's advantage is almost entirely from seeing FUTURE labels (+28.9 of +31 steps). When restricted to past-only labels, the oracle drops to +2.1 steps — only marginally better than hidden-state probes. This confirms the limitation is NOT that probes miss something in current hidden states, but that hidden states simply don't encode future safety trajectory. The mutual information between current hidden state and future labels (t+5 to t+15) is near-zero (0.043 bits), confirming there's no recoverable signal about the future. The implication: closing the gap requires either (a) future-label information (text monitoring), (b) multiple-step autoregressive hidden-state prediction, or (c) auxiliary training objectives that force the model to encode future safety trajectory.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"OpenThinker-7B evaluation\",\n    \"description\": \"4th model (Qwen2.5-7B base) to complete 2×2 architecture×scale matrix: {Llama-8B, Qwen2.5-7B} × {7-8B, 32B}. Controls for scale confound in cross-architecture comparison.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"openthinker_7b_static_coverage\": 0.512,\n      \"openthinker_7b_temporal_w10_coverage\": 0.654,\n      \"openthinker_7b_temporal_w15_coverage\": 0.641,\n      \"openthinker_7b_delta\": 0.142,\n      \"openthinker_7b_lead_mean\": -0.91,\n      \"openthinker_7b_fisher_orig\": 10.12,\n      \"openthinker_7b_fisher_pca3\": 2.37,\n      \"openthinker_7b_optimal_W\": 10,\n      \"scale_effect_llama\": \"8B→32B not applicable (different base arch)\",\n      \"scale_effect_qwen\": \"7B vs 32B — coverage +1.6pp static, +3.5pp temporal (minimal scale effect)\",\n      \"architecture_effect_at_7_8B\": \"Llama-8B vs Qwen2.5-7B: -4.7pp static, +12.6pp temporal delta difference\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 13: 2×2 Architecture × Scale Matrix\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Architecture\",\n          \"Scale\",\n          \"Static Cov\",\n          \"Temporal Cov\",\n          \"Delta\",\n          \"Optimal W\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"Llama-3.1\",\n            \"8B\",\n            0.465,\n            0.767,\n            \"+30.2pp\",\n            15\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OpenThinker-7B\",\n            \"Qwen2.5\",\n            \"7B\",\n            0.512,\n            0.654,\n            \"+14.2pp\",\n            10\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"Qwen2.5\",\n            \"32B\",\n            0.528,\n            0.689,\n            \"+16.1pp\",\n            10\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"Qwen2.5\",\n            \"32B\",\n            0.482,\n            0.648,\n            \"+16.6pp\",\n            10\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 2×2 matrix is clean: architecture determines temporal advantage magnitude (30pp Llama vs 14-17pp Qwen2.5), while scale has minimal effect within architecture. This eliminates a key confound and strengthens the characterization story. The paper can now claim that the temporal advantage difference is an architecture-level property, likely related to how safety signal is geometrically organized in the representation space.\\n\\nOpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base) confirms the architecture effect is NOT a scale confound. At matched scale (7-8B), the same pattern holds: Qwen2.5 has higher static coverage (51% vs 47%) but lower temporal delta (14pp vs 30pp). The optimal window is W=10 (same as Qwen2.5-32B), further confirming this is an architecture-level property. Scale effect within Qwen2.5 is minimal (+1.6pp static, +3.5pp temporal from 7B→32B), ruling out the hypothesis that 'Qwen2.5 only attenuates because 32B models have different dynamics.' The 2×2 matrix cleanly separates architecture effect (dominant) from scale effect (minimal), strengthening the architectural analysis contribution.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"GRU/Attention probe upper bound on 32B models\",\n    \"description\": \"Train GRU (hidden=256) and self-attention probes on r1-32b and QwQ-32B to verify whether complex architectures offer improvement over temporal LR on Qwen2.5 base models.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_gru_coverage\": 0.698,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": 0.89,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_acc\": 0.847,\n      \"r1_32b_attention_coverage\": 0.685,\n      \"r1_32b_attention_lead_mean\": 0.31,\n      \"qwq_gru_coverage\": 0.667,\n      \"qwq_gru_lead_mean\": -0.42,\n      \"qwq_attention_coverage\": 0.654,\n      \"qwq_attention_lead_mean\": -0.58,\n      \"gru_vs_lr_delta_r1_32b\": \"+1.9pp coverage\",\n      \"gru_vs_lr_delta_qwq\": \"+1.9pp coverage\",\n      \"gru_params\": 1622913,\n      \"attention_params\": 2105344,\n      \"lr_temporal_params\": 77025\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 14: Complex Probe Architectures on Qwen2.5 Models\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Params\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"Lead Mean\",\n          \"vs Temporal LR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"Temporal LR (W=10)\",\n            \"77K\",\n            0.689,\n            0.52,\n            \"baseline\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"GRU-256\",\n            \"1.6M\",\n            0.698,\n            0.89,\n            \"+0.9pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"Self-Attention\",\n            \"2.1M\",\n            0.685,\n            0.31,\n            \"-0.4pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"Temporal LR (W=10)\",\n            \"77K\",\n            0.667,\n            -0.73,\n            \"baseline\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"GRU-256\",\n            \"1.6M\",\n            0.667,\n            -0.42,\n            \"+0.0pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"Self-Attention\",\n            \"2.1M\",\n            0.654,\n            -0.58,\n            \"-1.3pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Complex probes don't help on Qwen2.5, confirming the 'simplicity is optimal' finding generalizes. The slightly larger GRU improvement on r1-32b (+0.9pp coverage, +0.37 lead) compared to QwQ (+0.0pp, +0.31 lead) is within noise. Key takeaway: the 30pp temporal advantage ceiling on Llama and 15-17pp ceiling on Qwen2.5 appear to be INFORMATION limits, not model capacity limits — better probes can't extract signal that isn't there.\\n\\nGRU provides marginal improvement (+1.9pp) over temporal LR on Qwen2.5, consistent with the r1-8b finding that complex architectures don't substantially help. The improvement is smaller than on Llama (where GRU gained ~3.7pp) because Qwen2.5's more concentrated signal is already well-captured by linear methods — the nonlinear capacity of GRU has less to exploit. Self-attention probe performs slightly WORSE than GRU (expected at this data scale — attention is data-hungry). The 'simplicity is optimal at current data scales' conclusion holds cross-architecture. The 20× parameter count of GRU (1.6M vs 77K) for <2pp improvement confirms the cost-benefit analysis favors temporal LR for practical deployment.\"\n  }\n]","numLines":907,"startLine":1,"totalLines":907}}}
