{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014yBPvn1zYCKv5jd5kYcMsj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — contrastive_trace_safety\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (LLM Security Track)\n5\t- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n6\t- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026 (9 days)\n7\t\n8\t## Core Claims (3 条)\n9\t\n10\t1. **Static hidden-state probes fail to reliably detect safety commitment in reasoning model traces** — static per-step probes (LogisticRegression, linear classifiers analogous to SafeSwitch/SIREN) achieve <50% detection coverage on the commitment detection task, despite hidden states containing strong safety-relevant signal (Fisher discriminant ratio 17-18 in full representation space).\n11\t\n12\t2. **Temporal window probes saturate oracle-achievable detection coverage** — a simple temporal window LogisticRegression (W=15 steps) achieves 76.7% detection coverage, matching the label-oracle upper bound. The +30pp improvement over static probes comes from capturing \"safety reasoning density decline\" patterns invisible to single-step classification.\n13\t\n14\t3. **Pre-textual detection of safety commitment is fundamentally limited in hidden-state space** — no hidden-state method (static, temporal window, or sequential GRU) achieves positive detection lead time. The oracle's +31-step advantage comes from directly observing label sequences; hidden states encode current safety state but not future safety trajectory. This establishes a principled limitation boundary for hidden-state monitoring approaches.\n15\t\n16\t## Story Spine\n17\t\n18\t- **Problem**: Reasoning models exhibit \"self-jailbreak\" — safety reasoning and harmful execution are interleaved throughout extended CoT (84% of traces contain safety labels after step 10), culminating in a \"commitment point\" where safety reasoning permanently ceases. Existing hidden-state jailbreak detectors (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN) are designed for instruction-tuned models where the attack is an adversarial INPUT; they have never been evaluated on reasoning-model self-jailbreak where the attack is the model's OWN reasoning process.\n19\t\n20\t- **Existing approaches & their gaps**: Hidden-state detectors achieve 95% defense rate on standard LLMs (TrajGuard) but operate at token-level granularity. Reasoning-model safety analysis (Self-Jailbreaking, HarmThoughts) characterizes the phenomenon but doesn't provide deployable detectors. Text-level CoT monitors (SafeRemind, SafeThink) are vulnerable to pathological CoT. No work bridges hidden-state detection TO reasoning-model self-jailbreak.\n21\t\n22\t- **Our framing**: We provide the first systematic evaluation of hidden-state detection methods on reasoning-model safety commitment, discovering that (a) the safety signal exists but is distributed across minor principal components (PCA top-3 explains only 17% variance, Fisher drops from 17→1), making geometric methods (Mahalanobis/TrajGuard) fail; (b) temporal context is critical for reliable detection but cannot anticipate future safety trajectory; (c) a simple temporal window probe is surprisingly optimal — complex architectures (GRU) offer no advantage at current data scales.\n23\t\n24\t- **Headline result template**: \"On HarmThoughts reasoning traces, temporal window probes achieve 76.7% detection coverage (matching oracle ceiling), while static probes achieve only 46.5% — but no method achieves pre-textual detection (best lead time = -0.2 steps vs oracle +31 steps).\"\n25\t\n26\t## Venue Fit Risks\n27\t\n28\t- **EMNLP LLM Security track fit**: Strong — defense/detection method for reasoning model jailbreak, directly addressing \"defense lags behind attack\" gap noted in track CFP.\n29\t- **Method simplicity risk**: Core method is temporal window LogisticRegression (not a novel architecture). Mitigate: frame as empirical study with method insight (\"simplicity IS the finding — complex temporal models don't help at this data scale\").\n30\t- **Negative finding risk**: Claim 3 (pre-textual detection impossible) could be seen as \"limitation\" not \"contribution.\" Mitigate: frame as establishing principled boundary — knowing what's NOT possible is valuable for practitioners deploying safety monitors.\n31\t- **Cross-architecture coverage**: MVP only tested on r1-8b (Llama-3.1 base). Must validate on Qwen2.5-based models (r1-32b, QwQ-32B) for the \"cross-architecture\" story. If temporal advantage doesn't hold cross-architecture → Claim 2 weakens to single-model.\n32\t\n33\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n34\t\n35\t1. \"Temporal window LR is trivial — where's the method novelty?\" → Primary contribution is empirical study; method insight is that simplicity matches oracle ceiling.\n36\t2. \"Only tested on one model family\" → Cross-architecture experiments (P0) will address.\n37\t3. \"Lead time is negative — what's the practical use?\" → Coverage matters for post-hoc audit; real-time monitoring with ≤0 lead time still allows intervention at commitment point (vs missing it entirely with static methods).\n38\t4. \"209 training traces is too few — results may not generalize\" → Cross-model validation + bootstrap CIs.\n39\t5. \"Why not use the full 16-label taxonomy for richer supervision?\" → Ablation planned (P1).\n40\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c514e65-084f-43fa-8596-877e1ee4e682","uuid":"65f1b38a-ee2b-4b0b-be86-5ba9826a4415","timestamp":"2026-05-16T20:02:53.047Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T194646/median/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — contrastive_trace_safety\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (LLM Security Track)\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026 (9 days)\n\n## Core Claims (3 条)\n\n1. **Static hidden-state probes fail to reliably detect safety commitment in reasoning model traces** — static per-step probes (LogisticRegression, linear classifiers analogous to SafeSwitch/SIREN) achieve <50% detection coverage on the commitment detection task, despite hidden states containing strong safety-relevant signal (Fisher discriminant ratio 17-18 in full representation space).\n\n2. **Temporal window probes saturate oracle-achievable detection coverage** — a simple temporal window LogisticRegression (W=15 steps) achieves 76.7% detection coverage, matching the label-oracle upper bound. The +30pp improvement over static probes comes from capturing \"safety reasoning density decline\" patterns invisible to single-step classification.\n\n3. **Pre-textual detection of safety commitment is fundamentally limited in hidden-state space** — no hidden-state method (static, temporal window, or sequential GRU) achieves positive detection lead time. The oracle's +31-step advantage comes from directly observing label sequences; hidden states encode current safety state but not future safety trajectory. This establishes a principled limitation boundary for hidden-state monitoring approaches.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: Reasoning models exhibit \"self-jailbreak\" — safety reasoning and harmful execution are interleaved throughout extended CoT (84% of traces contain safety labels after step 10), culminating in a \"commitment point\" where safety reasoning permanently ceases. Existing hidden-state jailbreak detectors (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN) are designed for instruction-tuned models where the attack is an adversarial INPUT; they have never been evaluated on reasoning-model self-jailbreak where the attack is the model's OWN reasoning process.\n\n- **Existing approaches & their gaps**: Hidden-state detectors achieve 95% defense rate on standard LLMs (TrajGuard) but operate at token-level granularity. Reasoning-model safety analysis (Self-Jailbreaking, HarmThoughts) characterizes the phenomenon but doesn't provide deployable detectors. Text-level CoT monitors (SafeRemind, SafeThink) are vulnerable to pathological CoT. No work bridges hidden-state detection TO reasoning-model self-jailbreak.\n\n- **Our framing**: We provide the first systematic evaluation of hidden-state detection methods on reasoning-model safety commitment, discovering that (a) the safety signal exists but is distributed across minor principal components (PCA top-3 explains only 17% variance, Fisher drops from 17→1), making geometric methods (Mahalanobis/TrajGuard) fail; (b) temporal context is critical for reliable detection but cannot anticipate future safety trajectory; (c) a simple temporal window probe is surprisingly optimal — complex architectures (GRU) offer no advantage at current data scales.\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"On HarmThoughts reasoning traces, temporal window probes achieve 76.7% detection coverage (matching oracle ceiling), while static probes achieve only 46.5% — but no method achieves pre-textual detection (best lead time = -0.2 steps vs oracle +31 steps).\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n- **EMNLP LLM Security track fit**: Strong — defense/detection method for reasoning model jailbreak, directly addressing \"defense lags behind attack\" gap noted in track CFP.\n- **Method simplicity risk**: Core method is temporal window LogisticRegression (not a novel architecture). Mitigate: frame as empirical study with method insight (\"simplicity IS the finding — complex temporal models don't help at this data scale\").\n- **Negative finding risk**: Claim 3 (pre-textual detection impossible) could be seen as \"limitation\" not \"contribution.\" Mitigate: frame as establishing principled boundary — knowing what's NOT possible is valuable for practitioners deploying safety monitors.\n- **Cross-architecture coverage**: MVP only tested on r1-8b (Llama-3.1 base). Must validate on Qwen2.5-based models (r1-32b, QwQ-32B) for the \"cross-architecture\" story. If temporal advantage doesn't hold cross-architecture → Claim 2 weakens to single-model.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n1. \"Temporal window LR is trivial — where's the method novelty?\" → Primary contribution is empirical study; method insight is that simplicity matches oracle ceiling.\n2. \"Only tested on one model family\" → Cross-architecture experiments (P0) will address.\n3. \"Lead time is negative — what's the practical use?\" → Coverage matters for post-hoc audit; real-time monitoring with ≤0 lead time still allows intervention at commitment point (vs missing it entirely with static methods).\n4. \"209 training traces is too few — results may not generalize\" → Cross-model validation + bootstrap CIs.\n5. \"Why not use the full 16-label taxonomy for richer supervision?\" → Ablation planned (P1).\n","numLines":40,"startLine":1,"totalLines":40}}}
